  {
   "evidence": "AdjointAction.lean:67-71 states `adjointDerivation_\u03b9` on the derivation only; lines 99-104 likewise; module docstring lines 28-33 promise the bracket characterisation. Mathlib's own pattern for derivation actions is `Derivation.bracket_eq_fun` (Mathlib/RingTheory/Derivation/Lie.lean:72) plus statement-level lemmas on the module side.",
   "file": "TauCeti/LinearAlgebra/SymmetricAlgebra/AdjointAction.lean",
   "fix": "Add `theorem lie_\u03b9 (x y : L) : \u2045x, \u03b9 R L y\u2046 = \u03b9 R L \u2045x, y\u2046` and `theorem lie_mul (x : L) (p q : S) : \u2045x, p * q\u2046 = p * \u2045x, q\u2046 + q * \u2045x, p\u2046` (both by `simp`), and phrase the degree lemma at bracket level too (`lie_mem_homogeneousSubmodule : p \u2208 homogeneousSubmodule R L n \u2192 \u2045x, p\u2046 \u2208 homogeneousSubmodule R L n`) so the instance is usable without unfolding to the derivation.",
   "issue": "The Lie-module instance is characterised only through `lie_eq_adjointDerivation` plus derivation lemmas; there is no statement of the action on generators or of its interaction with multiplication at the bracket level. The docstring (line 28-30) and PR description claim the characterisation `\u2045x, \u03b9 y\u2046 = \u03b9 \u2045x, y\u2046`, but `grep 'lie_\u03b9\\|\u2045x, \u03b9' AdjointAction.lean` finds no such lemma; a consumer (e.g. showing `LieModule.maxTrivSubmodule R L S` is closed under multiplication for the `Sym(L)^L` step) must route every `rw` through `adjointDerivation` and `Derivation.leibniz`.",
   "line": 83
  }
 ],
